---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Cluster from "../components/Cluster.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import cl from "../data/clusters.json";
const c = cl.clusters.kvarner;
const md = (x) => String(x ?? "")
  .replace(/\*\*(.+?)\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>")
  .replace(/«(.+?)»/g, "<em>«$1»</em>")
  .replace(/(^|[^*])\*([^*\n]+?)\*/g, "$1<em>$2</em>");
---
<Base
  title="The Kvarner households"
  eyebrow="Rijeka · Bakar · 1626 to 1855"
  dek="Eighty-two people of the name on the Kvarner, in nine households across two hundred and thirty years — the second-largest concentration this archive holds, and the one it has said least about. This page draws what is there, and marks every place a join is missing.">

  <section class="prose">
    <p>Rijeka is where the archive's <em>oldest</em> household sits — six children baptised between 1626 and 1632 — and it is also where the archive has done the least work. The reason is the shape of the material. Gračišće gave a line because the registers there name fathers; Rijeka gives a stack of households that never touch.</p>
    <p>Two things emerged when they were finally set in order, and neither had been noticed before.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="doors" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="door" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
      <div class="dn">The name is an office, not a farm — and now it is documented</div>
      <div class="dd">The Bakar father of 1849 is entered <em>Defranceschi Regii</em>, of the king. That was an inference until the Hungarian state's own paperwork was opened, and it holds a <strong>harbour master of Fiume</strong>, a draughtsman who drew the <strong>imperial salt office at Bakar in 1827</strong>, and two naval officers of the name.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="door" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre)">
      <div class="dn">The chain that almost closes</div>
      <div class="dd">Valentino fathering 1742–57, Giuseppe naming his first son Valentino in 1772, a Valentino fathering again 1794–1805. The grandfather-to-grandson pattern is exactly right — and the boy who would carry it was buried on 22 August 1775, aged two.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Rule name="arrows" label="Nine households, in the order the register made them" />

  <Cluster c={c} />

  <Rule name="arrows" label={c.officials.heading} />
  <p class="note" set:html={md(c.officials.note)}></p>
  <div class="offs">
    {c.officials.rows.map(r => (
      <article class="off">
        <div class="off-h">
          <span class="off-w">{r.who}</span>
          <span class="off-y">{r.when}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="off-t" set:html={md(r.what)}></p>
        <p class="off-s">{r.where}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:22px;border-left-color:var(--accent)" set:html={md(c.officials.close)}></p>

  <Rule name="arrows" label="Where this sits" />
  <div class="doors">
    <a class="door" href={u("/fiume-1626/")}>
      <div class="dn">Fiume, 1626</div>
      <div class="dd">The one household on this page that has been read on the page rather than taken from the index — the baptisms in the priest's own hand.</div>
      <div class="go">Read the entries →</div>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href={u("/trees/")}>
      <div class="dn">The trees</div>
      <div class="dd">How far each family of the name actually goes, and what form its evidence allows it to be drawn in.</div>
      <div class="go">All the families →</div>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href={u("/households/")}>
      <div class="dn">Households</div>
      <div class="dd">All 112 reconstructed couples, with every indexed child and the warning that goes with each.</div>
      <div class="go">Browse them →</div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:26px">Every household on this page is built from FamilySearch index entries. The spellings are the indexers'. Where a mother carries the father's surname, that is the index, not the marriage. Confidence is now split. <strong>The office is documented</strong> — the Hungarian state's own pension schedules, plan catalogue and military schematisms name the men and their posts, and those citations are given above. <strong>That any of them is the Franciscus of the 1836 and 1849 baptisms remains inferred</strong>, resting on <em>Ferencz</em> being Franciscus and on the dates fitting; a Bakar register would settle it. <strong>The Valentino chain stays inferred</strong> and is still broken by a burial.</p>

<style>
.offs{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(320px,1fr));gap:16px;margin-top:20px}
.off{border:1px solid var(--rule);border-left:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:2px;padding:13px 16px 14px}
.off-h{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:baseline;gap:10px}
.off-w{font-family:var(--serif);font-size:18px;color:var(--ink)}
.off-y{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-3);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.off-t{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;line-height:1.62;color:var(--ink-2);margin:7px 0 0}
.off-s{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--ink-3);margin:9px 0 0}
</style>

</Base>
